INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON RUHLIN V. NEW YORK LIFE INSURANCE CO.
Some of the questions posed by the Rules of Decision Act and left unanswered by Erie, such as What does “rules of decision” mean?, have taken decades to answer. But one of the questions, What does “trials at common law” mean?, took only seven days to answer.

Ruhlin v. New York Life Insurance Co., 304 U.S. 202 (1938), involved a dispute over benefits allegedly due under a life and disability insurance policy. In 1935, the insurance company, New York Life, refused to pay disability benefits to the insured, John Ruhlin, when he made a claim for permanent disability. New York Life then filed a complaint in federal court in Pennsylvania to rescind the insurance contract because of alleged misrepresentations made by Ruhlin in his application for insurance.

Whether or not New York Life could rescind the policy depended upon the interpretation of a single clause in the policy, called the “incontestability clause.” On appeal, the Third Circuit decided in favor of New York Life and held that the incontestability clause did not apply to claims for disability. This result differed from holdings of the Fourth Circuit and Ninth Circuit, which in other cases involving the same policy language had held that the incontestability clause did apply. In each of these cases, the federal courts had applied federal principles of equity, rather than following state court precedent.

Ruhlin filed a petition for certiorari with the Supreme Court. Despite having won in the court of appeals, New York Life did not oppose the petition, and the Supreme Court granted the petition in order to resolve the circuit split over the interpretation of the incontestability clause. This circuit split raised a question of federal law, because the circuit courts were each interpreting federal equity principles when deciding whether the policies could be rescinded.

After the petition was granted, however, the Court decided Erie. And one week later, on May 2, 1938, the Court handed down its decision in Ruhlin. It said:

1236
It was stated in Carpenter v. Providence Washington Insurance Co., 41 U.S. (16 Pet.) 495, 511 (1842), that questions concerning the proper construction of contracts of insurance are ‘questions of general commercial law,’ and that state decisions on the subject, though entitled to great respect, ‘cannot conclude the judgment of this court.’ * * * [But the] decision in Erie Railroad Co. v. Tompkins, 304 U.S. 64, decided April 25, 1938, * * * [holds that the] subject is now to be governed, even in the absence of state statute, by the decisions of the appropriate state court. The doctrine applies though the question of construction arises not in an action at law, but in a suit in equity.

Id. at 205.

Having held that Erie applies to suits in equity, the Court concluded that it could not decide the question of the proper interpretation of the incontestability clause, because that was not a question whose answer could be found in federal law. Rather, it was a question to be answered by looking to state law. Indeed, after Erie, there was no circuit split to resolve, as federal courts sitting in different states could very well reach different interpretations of the same policy language if those states’ courts would interpret the policies differently. Given this, the Court vacated and remanded the case with instructions for the lower courts to ascertain “the applicable principles of state law.” Id. at 207.

The court left open, though, the question of which state’s law to follow on remand. Presumably, a Pennsylvania court would choose to apply Pennsylvania law to the policy, which had been delivered to Ruhlin in Pennsylvania. But of course, which law a Pennsylvania court chooses to apply when interpreting an insurance policy is itself a question of Pennsylvania law. Should federal courts also follow state courts to answer the question of which state’s law to follow? The Supreme Court did not opine on the answer to that question. Rather, it merely noted: “Under the general doctrine the interpretation of an insurance contract depends on the law of the place where the policy is delivered. We do not now determine which principle must be enforced if the Pennsylvania courts follow a different conflict of laws rule.”

The Court’s opinion in Ruhlin did not explain why Erie applied to a case in equity, notwithstanding that the RDA expressly applies only to “trials at common law.” Seven years later, in York, the Court finally explained why.
